        "id": "i2_l3",
        "title": "Wildcards & The PECS Principle",
        "xp": 40,
        "estimatedMinutes": 25,
        "story": "Scribes transporting soul vials across the valley must know whether a chest is meant to supply potions or consume raw ingredients. Java resolves generic subtyping variance using wildcards (?) and the PECS rule: Producer extends, Consumer super.",
        "explanation": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "In Java, generic types are **invariant**: `List<Integer>` is NOT a subtype of `List<Number>`. To allow flexible polymorphism in API arguments, Java provides **wildcards** (`?`)."
          },
          {
            "type": "code",
            "content": "The PECS Rule (Producer Extends, Consumer Super):",
            "code": "// Producer Extends: Read data FROM the collection (out)\npublic static double sumList(List<? extends Number> list) {\n    double sum = 0;\n    for (Number n : list) sum += n.doubleValue(); // Safe read\n    return sum;\n}\n\n// Consumer Super: Write data INTO the collection (in)\npublic static void addNumbers(List<? super Integer> list) {\n    list.add(42); // Safe write: list accepts Integers\n}"
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "calloutType": "tip",
            "content": "**PECS Principle (Joshua Bloch)**: If a parameterized type represents a *Producer* (you only read from it), use `? extends T`. If it represents a *Consumer* (you only write into it), use `? super T`."
          }
        ],
        "slides": [
          {
            "title": "Generics are Invariant",
            "content": "`List<Integer>` cannot be passed to a method expecting `List<Number>` because you could insert a `Double` and corrupt the list."
          },
          {
            "title": "Upper-Bounded Wildcard (? extends T)",
            "content": "Allows any subtype of T. You can read elements as `T`, but you cannot add elements (except null)."
          },
          {
            "title": "Lower-Bounded Wildcard (? super T)",
            "content": "Allows any supertype of T. You can safely add elements of type `T`, but reading returns `Object`."
          }
        ],
        "underTheHood": "Wildcard bounds are enforced during compiler type checking. When compiling `list.add(x)` on `List<? extends Number>`, the compiler knows the list holds some unknown subtype of `Number`, but cannot verify if that subtype is `Integer`, `Float`, or `BigDecimal`. Because it cannot prove type safety, it rejects the write at compile time.",
        "workedExample": {
          "code": "import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Transfer {\n    // PECS in action: src produces, dest consumes\n    public static <T> void copy(List<? extends T> src, List<? super T> dest) {\n        for (T item : src) {\n            dest.add(item);\n        }\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<Integer> ints = List.of(1, 2, 3);\n        List<Number> nums = new ArrayList<>();\n        copy(ints, nums); // Integer extends Number\n        System.out.println(nums);\n    }\n}",
          "walkthrough": [
            "Line 5: src uses ? extends T (Producer: read items).",
            "Line 5: dest uses ? super T (Consumer: write items).",
            "Line 11: copy(ints, nums) cleanly copies Integer elements into a Number collection."
          ]
        },
