        "id": "i7_l5",
        "title": "ACID Transactions & Rollback Strategies",
        "xp": 40,
        "estimatedMinutes": 25,
        "story": "When trading 1,000 gold emeralds for an ancient relic, you cannot risk the gold disappearing from your vault while the relic never appears in your inventory due to a sudden power failure. Database transactions guarantee that all changes succeed together or are rolled back completely.",
        "explanation": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "A **Transaction** is a sequence of SQL operations treated as a single atomic unit of work satisfying **ACID** properties:\n- **A**tomicity: All operations succeed, or all are undone.\n- **C**onsistency: State transitions preserve all database invariants and constraints.\n- **I**solation: Concurrent transactions execute without interfering with one another.\n- **D**urability: Once committed, changes survive system crashes and power loss."
          },
          {
            "type": "code",
            "content": "Managing Transactions in JDBC:",
            "code": "try (Connection con = dataSource.getConnection()) {\n    con.setAutoCommit(false); // 1. Start transaction\n    try {\n        debitAccount(con, fromId, amount);\n        creditAccount(con, toId, amount);\n        con.commit(); // 2. Commit all changes atomically\n    } catch (Exception ex) {\n        con.rollback(); // 3. Roll back on any failure!\n        throw ex;\n    } finally {\n        con.setAutoCommit(true); // 4. Restore default state\n    }\n}"
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "calloutType": "warning",
            "content": "Always restore `con.setAutoCommit(true)` in the `finally` block before returning a pooled connection, or the next thread borrowing it will inherit manual transaction mode!"
          }
        ],
        "slides": [
          {
            "title": "The ACID Guarantee",
            "content": "Atomicity ensures multi-table transfers never leave data in a half-finished state."
          },
          {
            "title": "setAutoCommit(false)",
            "content": "By default, JDBC auto-commits every individual statement. Disabling auto-commit begins an explicit multi-statement transaction."
          },
          {
            "title": "Commit vs Rollback",
            "content": "Call `con.commit()` when all operations succeed.\nCall `con.rollback()` inside catch blocks to discard all pending changes."
          }
        ],
        "underTheHood": "When auto-commit is disabled, modifications are held in the database server's transaction undo log and Write-Ahead Log (WAL). During `commit()`, a commit record is flushed to persistent disk storage (fsync). If `rollback()` occurs, the engine uses undo logs to reverse all uncommitted mutations.",
        "workedExample": {
          "code": "public class TxDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Transaction template:\");\n        System.out.println(\"1. setAutoCommit(false)\");\n        System.out.println(\"2. execute updates\");\n        System.out.println(\"3. commit() or rollback()\");\n    }\n}",
          "walkthrough": [
            "Demonstrates standard JDBC transaction lifecycle stages."
          ]
        },
        "commonMistakes": [
          {
            "mistake": "Catching an exception during a transaction without calling con.rollback()",
            "error": "Uncommitted locks and partial changes linger until connection timeout or pool return",
            "fix": "Always call `con.rollback()` inside the catch block."
          },
          {
            "mistake": "Assuming DDL statements like CREATE TABLE can always be rolled back",
            "error": "In databases like MySQL and Oracle, DDL triggers an implicit commit, breaking rollback",
            "fix": "Perform schema migrations separately from transactional application business logic."
          }
        ],
        "checkpoint": [
          {
            "question": "Which JDBC method begins an explicit multi-statement transaction?",
            "options": [
              "connection.setAutoCommit(false)",
              "connection.startTransaction()",
              "connection.begin()",
              "connection.lock()"
            ],
            "answer": 0,
            "explanation": "Setting `autoCommit` to `false` disables single-statement commits, starting an explicit transaction."
          },
          {
            "question": "What does the 'A' in ACID stand for?",
            "options": [
              "Atomicity (all operations complete or none do)",
              "Asynchronous",
              "Authorization",
              "Allocation"
            ],
            "answer": 0,
            "explanation": "Atomicity guarantees all-or-nothing execution of the operations in a transaction."
          }
        ],
